# The asset pipeline reads source files byte for byte, and names what it cooks from their contents
# (docs/adr/0069-the-asset-pipeline.md): no line-ending conversion on checkout, whatever the
# platform's settings, and metadata files with LF endings everywhere.
*.glb binary
*.gltf -text
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.meta text eol=lf
# The fonts of uwui's tests, which hold binary tables.
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
# The texts of uwui's tests, which the tests break into lines: a carriage return would be a
# line break of its own.
crates/uwui/tests/texts/*.txt text eol=lf
# The list of the sample game's source assets, which scripts/fetch-assets.sh reads line by line
# and field by field: a carriage return would end up in its last field.
*.tsv text eol=lf
# The engine's documents, which its writer gives the same bytes on every platform, LF endings
# included (docs/adr/0086-serialization.md): a checkout keeps them as written. A level written back
# by the editor is so the file written by hand, byte for byte, when nothing in it changed
# (docs/adr/0134-the-editors-core.md).
*.toml text eol=lf
*.scene text eol=lf
# uwui's documents, in KDL (docs/adr/0241-every-document-of-uwui-in-kdl-read-by-its-own-reader.md):
# a checkout keeps them as written, so that their errors name the lines that their author sees, and
# their text, which is their cooked form, is the same on every platform. KDL's own tests are kept byte for byte: some test a carriage return or a byte order mark.
*.kdl text eol=lf
# uwui's fixtures of verdicts, which hold documents of KDL as they were read.
crates/uwui/tests/reference/verdicts/*.txt text eol=lf
# The layouts that the editor kept in TOML before they were KDL, kept as fixtures as it wrote them.
crates/slime-editor/tests/reference/layouts/* text eol=lf
crates/uwui-kdl/tests/kdl-suite/** -text
